WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.970
[DIGITAL EFFECT]

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:05.820
SPEAKER 1: (SINGING)
Your birthday, boy.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.630
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Google
Kubernetes Engine turns five!

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:09.130
Yeah!

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:11.580
Happy birthday, GKE.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.710
What is GKE?

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:16.070
It is the first cloud-based
managed Kubernetes service.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:19.100
And as we celebrate
GKE's fifth birthday,

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.290
let's take a little journey
through the five things

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.080
that we all love
so much about it.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.810
First off, GKE is easy to use.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.530
We all know that Kubernetes
is the de facto standard

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:33.280
for container orchestration.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.110
But we also know that it can get
complex to operate and manage.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.910
That's where GKE comes in.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.300
It takes away the complexity
out of Kubernetes,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.180
by providing a robust command
line interface and easy

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.170
to use UI to manage all your
containers in one place.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.140
It manages DNS, networking,
traffic management,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:54.850
and load balancing,
amongst other things.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.450
And it works across a lot of
simple and complex use cases,

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.800
including Linux, Windows,
stateful, stateless,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:07.300
existing or new apps and single
or multi tier cluster apps.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.490
Second is trust.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.440
Once you develop
and deploy the app,

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.070
you have complete control to
manage that GKE environment.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.990
You get the choice
to automatically

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.230
upgrade and repair them
if something goes wrong,

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.260
without worrying about
availability, because Google

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.090
site reliability engineers
constantly monitor

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.780
your cluster and its
computing, networking,

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.700
and storage resources.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.740
With release channels,
you get the freedom

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.050
to choose the Kubernetes
version of your choice.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.050
You can choose the
cutting-edge latest release

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.890
version in the Rapid channel, or
choose the most tested version

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:45.300
in the Stable channel.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.080
Or be in between in
the Regular channel.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:53.520
Next is best price
to performance.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:56.670
When you use GKE, you get
the best value for your money

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.630
because it does offer the
lowest cost to performance ratio

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.060
for running the
containerized applications.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.510
It offers four-way
industry's first auto-scaling

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.930
to optimize resources
when you scale up and down

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.490
your apps based on your needs.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:14.880
For advanced apps, you can
use application accelerators,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.620
like GPUs and TPUs on GKE.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.850
And it helps kickstart your
hybrid and multi-cloud journey.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.690
Next, and very
important, is BeyondProd,

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:28.290
the cloud-native
security approach.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:32.760
With strong security defaults,
GKE offers cluster security

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.660
beyond platform hardening
and OS patching.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:39.300
The clusters in GKE natively
support Kubernetes network

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.560
policy to restrict traffic
with pod level firewall rules.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.540
You can verify,
enforce and improve

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.570
the security of infrastructure
components and packages used

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.610
for your container images.

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.100
With the sandbox feature,
you get a second layer

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.470
of defense between
containerized workloads

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.050
on GKE for enhanced
workload security.

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:01.890
You also get built-in
support for a wide range

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.360
of compliance standards.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.060
In short, you get
BeyondProd security,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.140
Google's cloud-native security
approach for your workloads.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.590
And we are on number five.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.760
What's needed to
navigate change?

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.520
The only constant
in our lives, scale.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:22.340
Whether you want to simplify
your infrastructure management,

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.150
have batch processing
needs, or simply looking

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.530
to run internet scale apps and
absorb large spikes in traffic,

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.690
with GKE, you are in luck.

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.960
Because starting with
1.18, GKE provides

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:39.520
the most supported nodes of any
cloud-based Kubernetes service.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.980
And three times
the number of nodes

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:44.680
supported by open
source Kubernetes.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.230
How's that for five in five?

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.535
